# Example config file for agent-bwrap.
#
# Copy this file, fill in the paths for your deployment, and point
# AGENT_BWRAP_CONFIG at the copy — or just set these as plain environment
# variables and skip the file entirely. Both work identically; the wrapper
# sources this file (if AGENT_BWRAP_CONFIG is set) before reading its
# environment, so either can supply any given variable.

# --- Required ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Absolute path to the real agent binary to run inside the sandbox.
AGENT_BWRAP_CMD=/usr/local/bin/my-agent-cli

# Absolute path to the read-write workspace directory the agent is confined
# to. Created if it does not already exist.
AGENT_BWRAP_WORKSPACE=/home/agent-runner/workspace

# --- Optional ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Path to bwrap. Defaults to "bwrap" resolved from PATH. On a host that
# restricts unprivileged user-namespace creation (see apparmor/), point this
# at the AppArmor-profiled copy instead, e.g.:
# BWRAP_BIN=/usr/local/lib/agent-bwrap/bwrap

# Colon-separated extra read-write binds: the agent's own config/data/cache
# directories, a scratch directory, package-manager caches — anything the
# agent needs to write besides the workspace itself.
# AGENT_BWRAP_RW=/home/agent-runner/.cache/my-agent:/home/agent-runner/.config/my-agent:/tmp/agent-scratch

# Colon-separated extra read-only binds: typically git/SSH credentials so the
# agent can fetch, push, or open a PR from inside the sandbox. Anything listed
# here is visible in the sandbox read-only — bind only what the agent needs.
# See README.md's threat model before binding credential material.
# AGENT_BWRAP_RO=/home/agent-runner/.gitconfig:/home/agent-runner/.ssh:/home/agent-runner/.config/gh

# Colon-separated additions to the built-in read-only /etc allowlist. Rarely
# needed — the default list already covers DNS/TLS resolution, the dynamic
# linker, and common shell-startup files.
# AGENT_BWRAP_SYSTEM_RO=/etc/some-extra-file

# Set to 1 to skip the built-in /etc allowlist entirely and use only
# AGENT_BWRAP_SYSTEM_RO. Most deployments should leave this unset.
# AGENT_BWRAP_NO_SYSTEM_DEFAULTS=1
